you guys may have noticed that most of  our iPad videos are done by vadim it's  not like I don't like iPads I just like  Mac books better but after this new  release and the new software updates I  decided to finally give up my new  MacBook Air that I've been using for a  while and give the new iPad pro ago in  this video I'm gonna share with you all  the real-world differences that jumped  out at me after being switched over for  a bit something I was expecting while  others I definitely wasn't and there  were surprises and along with that I'll  let you guys know if I'm switching to  the iPad pro for my basic daily uses  let's start out with something that I've  been pleading for in my Mac books for a  while now and that is face ID now  logging in is pretty quick you just have  to swipe up maybe with the new keyboard  they're gonna change that so it's  automatic but where it's super nice to  have is being in Safari and using Safari  autofill and in apps where it just logs  in automatically the next thing that I  notice is that the touch screen is  surprisingly useful now at first that  was kind of weird just pecking at the  screen all the time but I did have to  use it because I don't have a trackpad  and I did find that for certain things  it is quite handy one of those is being  able to just move my pointer if I'm  writing up a document exactly where I  want it pretty much instantly by  touching I also used it for scrolling  and for gestures and I know those of you  guys that are using Windows laptops your  guys are used to this but I'm personally  not because Mac books don't have  touchscreens  and it was quite comfortable and handy  to do so and think of another part of  that was the iPad pros 120 Hertz screen  which makes it just feel so fluid to be  able to scroll through pages now the  first day I kept accidentally trying to  reach down for the trackpad and move my  screen around and get my pointer but  obviously it is not there on this  keyboard but as you guys know that's  gonna be changing really soon  I used the bridge Pro keyboard and even  though it's the best option available  right now I have to be honest and say  that it does not compare to the new  magic keyboard in the MacBook Air one  thing that really bugged me was deleting  sentences when I'm writing because it  just goes at one constant speed and it  is just annoying and another thing is  trying to select portions  of text by touching and then hitting the  select there's just much quicker having  a track read for that even though the  keys field II said they're just a bit  too small and for long typing sessions  my fingers feel too cramped and get  tired the display also felt a bit  cramped now I have the 11 inch version  but if you're trying to replace a  MacBook I would suggest a 12-point 9  inch version thankfully the iPad  displays use a more squared aspect ratio  and just like the keyboard for most  tasks I got used to it now even though  the display is smaller that doesn't mean  that the display is worse in fact for  many things the display is much better  the iPad screen can display more colors  which is better if you want to edit  images or video but that didn't matter  to me what did matter was the display  brightness the MacBook Air only reaches  350 minutes so that means if you're in a  bright room where you're outdoors that  could be limiting the iPad on the other  hand reaches 600 nits that's almost  twice as bright and it's definitely  handy at times heck even the best Mac  the pros only reach 500 nits the other  thing that I noticed the difference in  was playing back video I noticed that  the quad speakers sound more immersive  when watching movies but they are  quieter so that was a downside  especially if my kids are running around  another difference is that even though  the display has almost twice as much  pixels as a 1080p screen you can't  select worke in YouTube which I do all  the time on the MacBook Air for better  quality  now 1080p looks fine on an 11 inch  screen and also saves battery but that  would be an even bigger bummer if you  bought the 12 point 9 inch iPad now talk  about battery life I did notice some  differences I would say probably roughly  one to maybe two hours less real-world  uses for mixed productivity tasks appear  to the MacBook Air but along with that I  was pushing the display you know with  higher brightness level so that does  have something to do with it now if  you're somebody that does a lot of web  conferencing or facetiming the 7  megapixel camera on the iPad pro  absolutely destroys the 720p webcam on  the MacBook Air which equates to roughly  1.2 megapixels one issue did come up  though  if you're using the iPad as a laptop  with the keyboard attached the  front-facing camera not only is on the  left-hand side but it's also lowered  down so when you're talking to somebody  if you're looking at the screen and  looking at the person it looks like  you're looking off to the side and kind  of ignoring them and which is much less  of an issue on the MacBook Air now of  course with the iPad you can always take  it out of the case or your keyboard and  flip it up and hold it in your hand but  if you need to be able to do some  researching some typing at the same time  obviously most of the keyboard cases  they do not offer that orientation I  also want to mention the rear cameras  obviously MacBooks don't have those and  even though I'm not somebody who's gonna  take photos and videos or with these and  they actually turn out quite good as far  as quality I did use it to scan a  document and that is very convenient  being able to do that you also get lidar  for augmented reality and that might be  convenient for some people but I just  didn't use that and one thing that is  missing on the iPad is a headphone jack  now personally most of the time I end up  using my ear pods anyway so it's not  something that was an inconvenience to  me there's not many ways to quantify the  performance difference between these two  but I use browser bench in Safari and  the iPad scored a hundred and fifty  compared to the already quite high score  of 112 that the MacBook Air got now in  the real world the iPad did feel a  little bit more snappy but that could  have just been that 120 Hertz screen  that makes everything feel super quick  the other thing I never got was heat on  my lap which is sometimes noticeable  after using the MacBook Air for a while  comparing a few more metrics in  Geekbench 5 the iPad scores a massive  4600 88 compared to my best ever score  with the air that was 3934 and that's a  score I got when I liquid cooled my  macbook air out of the box they got 3231  while running quite hot there's also an  i7 option for the MacBook Air but it  doesn't run much faster because the  MacBook Air has an adequate cooling for  these quad-core processors the other  performance difference is Wi-Fi the new  iPad pro supports Wi-Fi six  and in the toughest section of my house  it still managed 192 megabit per second  out of my maximum 250 whereas the  MacBook Air only got 96 because it only  supports Wi-Fi 5 on the graphic side  that the story is similar my ears new g7  graphics scored high but the iPad is  even better at almost 10,000 medal score  now most people who buy a MacBook Air  aren't needing the greatest graphics but  just so you know more and more regular  normal apps are using graphics  acceleration now for example photo  editing I did some photo editing using  Lightroom and while the air does a  decent job the iPad is actually smoother  in general many tasks feel faster on the  iPad which is likely due to apps being  more optimized because they are built  from the ground up to support metal and  the processors in these iPads that is  something that I have to touch on the  apps applications were the thing that  was tying me most to Mac OS and Mac  books but in the last year Apple finally  gave us a usable file system with  external drive support and many  companies have really stepped it up with  powerful apps like Photoshop luma fusion  and affinity zaps in fact it seems like  more companies now are putting their  main focus on iPad apps and mobile apps  not only do we have lots of productivity  app options but they are often less  expensive while being really good for  example Pixelmator photo which could be  bought for just $5 in some cases they  actually offer a better experience than  on Mac OS for example using Google Drive  is so much quicker and snappier as an  app on the iPad and along with you know  other apps that they offer and on top of  that if you're somebody that wants to  play games you are much much better off  with an iPad pro compared to a MacBook  Air of course the MacBook Air does have  exclusive apps such as Final Cut which  is not available for the iPad pro at  least just yet but I think overall if we  look at applications and prices the iPad  definitely has the lake up and with that  we haven't even touched on Penn support  now I'm not somebody that uses the Apple  pencil very often I don't draw  don't do things like that but if you're  somebody that likes to design you like  to draw sketch right obviously that is  something you can do with the iPad Pro  and you cannot do with the macbook air  overall I was very impressed with how  well the iPad pro does when being used  as a MacBook Air replacement no it is  not perfect but in most use cases that  you would use that iPad air for this  does it close to as well and in some  cases even better and along with that it  has much much more capabilities for  other use cases you know the cameras the  screen drawing gaming all of that stuff  but you simply can't do on the MacBook  Air after using it as a Mac replacement  I have even less doubt that if you're  only gonna buy one device you should  absolutely get the iPad pro now  obviously I am NOT in that camp  I have a ton of different tech devices  so will I be switching from my MacBook  to an iPad no at least not yet there's  still something great about a standard  well balanced clamshell notebook  especially one like the MacBook Air that  has a fantastic keyboard and trackpad  what I do mostly with my basic device is  web browsing research and scripting and  the MacBook Air just does a better job  at those things iPad software has  finally caught up and it is good enough  but the hardware is just not there yet  which is why I'm really intrigued with  that keyboard now there are gonna be  other options like the new bridge that  has a trackpad but I am guessing that  Apple's is going to be the best one I  think that floating design is going to  be amazing allowing the 11 inch size to  be more usable and it will help in  cramped spaces that extra USB C port  will be nice to charge or for using a  headphone adapter while leaving the  other one available the biggest thing  for me is the keyboard and if it  actually is the same keyboard that is in  the new MacBook Air that will solve the  keyboard issue and it will finally make  the iPad pro be a macbook air killer but  for now I am sticking to my Mac if  you've switched let me know your  experience down in the comments section  below and if you guys want to get the  best deal on a new MacBook  err or maybe an iPad check out the links  in the video description because I want  to see more videos click that circle  above or if you want to see a couple  great videos we have some right over  there this Ben Max and I will see you in  the next video  [Music]

